\section{Driving force versus decay: the escape race}
\label{sec:theory}

We now solve the near-collapse dynamics in the setting where \cref{ass:aligned} holds with an isotropic predictor, $\Pred_0=p_0I$, the case the degenerate-predictor discussion of \cref{sec:setup} singles out.
Statements labelled Proposition or Corollary are exact.
The survival criterion of \cref{sec:race} is an approximation; we measure its accuracy rather than prove it (\cref{sec:experiments}).
Proofs are in \cref{app:proofs}.

\subsection{Exact structure}

\begin{proposition}[Exact mode reduction; per-mode system of \citealp{tian2021understanding}]
\label{prop:reduction}
Let \cref{ass:aligned}(i) hold, $d\ge n$, $\Pred_0=p_0I$, $\Wc_0=\sum_k a_k(0)\,\tilde u_ku_k^\top$ with orthonormal $\tilde u_k\in\R^d$, and $\Wt_0=\Wc_0$.
Then for all $t$, $\Wc=\sum_ka_k\tilde u_ku_k^\top$, $\Wt=\sum_k\bar a_k\tilde u_ku_k^\top$ and $\Pred=\sum_kp_k\tilde u_k\tilde u_k^\top+p_\perp\big(I-\sum_k\tilde u_k\tilde u_k^\top\big)$, where
\begin{equation}
\dot a_k=p_kc_k\bar a_k-\big(p_k^2\sigma_k+\lambda_W\big)a_k,\qquad
\dot p_k=\alpha\big[(c_k\bar a_k-p_k\sigma_ka_k)\,a_k-\lambda_Pp_k\big],\qquad
\dot{\bar a}_k=\kappa(a_k-\bar a_k),
\label{eq:reduced}
\end{equation}
and $\dot p_\perp=-\alpha\lambda_Pp_\perp$.
With the copy target ($\bar a_k=a_k$),
\begin{equation}
\dot a_k=a_k\,g_k(p_k),\qquad
g_k(p)=\underbrace{p\,c_k}_{\text{drive}}-\underbrace{\big(p^2\sigma_k+\lambda_W\big)}_{\text{decay}},\qquad
\dot p_k=\alpha\big[(c_k-p_k\sigma_k)\,a_k^2-\lambda_Pp_k\big].
\label{eq:growth}
\end{equation}
\end{proposition}
This is Tian et al.'s per-mode system (their eqs.~11--12, with $w^2\leftrightarrow a_k^2$, $\tau\leftrightarrow c_k$, $1+\sigma^2\leftrightarrow\sigma_k$), here with anisotropic $(c_k,\sigma_k)$ and an EMA target.
Each data direction $u_k$ is thus carried by one amplitude $a_k$ and one predictor gain $p_k$, and the rank of $\Wc$ is the number of amplitudes that survive.
Because $p_k$ is of order one at initialisation, the drive is first order in the amplitude.
A direction can grow only while its gain lies in the \emph{window}
\begin{equation}
g_k(p)>0\iff p\in\big(p_-^k,p_+^k\big),\qquad p_\pm^k=\frac{c_k\pm\sqrt{c_k^2-4\lambda_W\sigma_k}}{2\sigma_k},
\label{eq:window}
\end{equation}
which is non-empty if and only if $c_k^2>4\lambda_W\sigma_k$.
A gain that is too small supplies too little drive; a gain that is too large makes the self-prediction decay $p^2\sigma_k$ win.

