\begin{proposition}[Fixed points and their stability]
\label{prop:fixed}
Take the copy target and $\lambda_W,\lambda_P>0$.
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*),nosep]
\item The collapsed point $(a_k,p_k)=(0,0)$ is locally stable for every $k$.
\item Direction $k$ has a non-collapsed fixed point if and only if $c_k^2\ge4\lambda_W\sigma_k$. For $c_k^2>4\lambda_W\sigma_k$ there are two, at $p=p_\pm^k$ (of the sign of $c_k$) with $a^{2}=\lambda_Pp/(c_k-p\sigma_k)$; the one at $p_-^k$ is a saddle and the one at $p_+^k$ is stable. At equality they merge into a saddle-node, which is not robustly attracting. Hence the final rank is at most $\#\{k:c_k^2\ge4\lambda_W\sigma_k\}$.
\item If $\lambda_W>0$ but $\lambda_P=0$, no direction has a non-collapsed fixed point: $\dot p_k=0$ with $a_k\neq0$ forces the least-squares gain $p_k=c_k/\sigma_k$, where $g_k=-\lambda_W<0$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}
Part (b) is the static threshold of \citet{tian2021understanding}, \citet{wang2021directset} and \citet{arbel2026peira} in our notation (their $\eta>\tau^2/(4(1+s^2))$ and $c^2\ge4\lambda$), which we do not claim.
Part (c) isolates a role of the predictor's weight decay: at the least-squares gain $c_k/\sigma_k$, to which $p_k$ is pulled when $\lambda_P=0$, drive and self-prediction decay cancel, so a surviving direction needs a predictor shrunk below it.

\begin{corollary}[Exact scalar race for $\alpha=1$]
\label{cor:alpha1}
Under \cref{prop:reduction} with the copy target, $\alpha=1$ and $\lambda_W=\lambda_P=\lambda$, the identity \eqref{eq:balance} restricted to direction $k$ gives $p_k^2-a_k^2=e^{-2\lambda t}\big(p_0^2-a_k(0)^2\big)$, so
\begin{equation}
\dot a_k=g_k(p_k)\,a_k,\qquad p_k=\sqrt{a_k^2+e^{-2\lambda t}\big(p_0^2-a_k(0)^2\big)} ,
\label{eq:alpha1}
\end{equation}
a closed scalar equation, exact at every amplitude and for any initial imbalance (it is the per-mode integral of \citealp{tian2021understanding}, their eq.~14).
In the balanced case $p_0=a_k(0)$ it is autonomous, $\dot a=(|a|c_k-a^2\sigma_k-\lambda)\,a$, and direction $k$ survives if and only if $|a_k(0)|>p_-^k\approx\lambda/c_k$.
\end{corollary}
The balanced case is the basin of \citet{tian2021understanding}. At a standard initialisation, however, $a_k(0)\ll p_0=O(1)$, and the identity only says that the gain decays at the rate of weight decay until the amplitude has grown to its size; which comes first is the race we now quantify.

\subsection{The race in the unbalanced regime}
\label{sec:race}

\Cref{prop:fixed} says which end states exist, not which one training reaches; with $\lambda_W,\lambda_P>0$ both the collapsed point and the survivors are attractors, and which one training reaches depends on the side of the separatrix through the saddle at $p_-^k$ on which it starts. For a start at $a_k(0)\ll\min(1,p_0)$ and $p_k(0)=p_0$ the race below locates that boundary.
As long as $a_k^2(c_k-p\sigma_k)\ll\lambda_Pp$, the data term in $\dot p_k$ is negligible and the gain decays freely, $p(t)=p_0e^{-\alpha\lambda_Pt}$.
For $\alpha=1$ this is the small-amplitude limit of \cref{cor:alpha1}; for $\alpha\neq1$ it is an approximation.
The amplitude then accumulates the integrated net rate along the decaying gain (\cref{fig:drive-decay}b)
\begin{equation}
\log\frac{a_k(t)}{a_k(0)}\approx F_k(t)=\int_0^tg_k\big(p(s)\big)\,ds=\frac{c_k\big(p_0-p\big)-\tfrac{\sigma_k}{2}\big(p_0^2-p^2\big)}{\alpha\lambda_P}-\lambda_Wt .
\label{eq:F}
\end{equation}
$F_k$ grows while the gain is inside the window, so it peaks when the gain leaves it at $p_-^k$, or at $t=0$ if an initial shrinking phase outweighs that growth.
If $p_0>p_+^k$, the direction first shrinks while the gain is above the window and then grows inside it; $F_k$ counts both phases.

\paragraph{Capture.}
Direction $k$ takes over its gain once its data drive on $p_k$ balances weight decay, that is once it crosses the nullcline $\dot p_k=0$ of \eqref{eq:growth}, $a^2=\lambda_Pp/(c_k-p\sigma_k)$.
Above the nullcline the gain is pulled up towards $p_+^k$, and the direction converges to the stable fixed point of \cref{prop:fixed}(b).
Capture helps only while the direction still grows ($p\ge p_-^k$); no constant is fitted.

\begin{definition}[Race criterion; an approximation]
\label{def:race}
Direction $k$ is predicted to survive if and only if
\begin{equation}
R_k=\max_{t\le T:\;p(t)\ge p_-^k}\Big[\log a_k(0)+F_k(t)-\tfrac12\log\frac{\lambda_P\,p(t)}{c_k-p(t)\,\sigma_k}\Big]\;\ge\;0 .
\label{eq:race}
\end{equation}
where $T$ is the training horizon.
The predicted rank is $\hat r=\#\{k:R_k\ge0\}$, which for $\Pred_0=p_0I$ is the matching rank of \cref{sec:setup}.
For $\lambda_P=0$ the gain does not drift, and direction $k$ is predicted to survive if $g_k(p_0)>0$ and $\log a_k(0)+g_k(p_0)\,T\ge\log\theta$, where $\theta$ is the amplitude at which a direction is counted in the rank; with $\lambda_W>0$ this branch is a statement about $t\le T$ only, since \cref{prop:fixed}(c) excludes a non-collapsed fixed point.
\end{definition}
The criterion approximates the separatrix through the saddle of \cref{prop:fixed}(b), and it depends only on $(c_k,\sigma_k)$ and $(p_0,a_k(0),\alpha,\lambda_W,\lambda_P,T)$.
Without weight decay, survival reduces to the initial window, $g_k(p_0)>0$, up to the finite horizon.
With weight decay it is neither necessary nor sufficient: a gain above the window can decay into it, and a direction inside it still loses if its gain falls below $p_-^k$ before capture.

\paragraph{Critical weight decay.}
Evaluating the capture term at $p_-^k\approx\lambda_W/c_k$ and expanding for small weight decay, with $\lambda_W=\lambda_P=\lambda$ and $\alpha=1$, direction $k$ survives when
\begin{equation}
h_k(p_0)\equiv c_kp_0-\tfrac12\sigma_kp_0^2\;\gtrsim\;\lambda\log\frac{p_0}{a_0},\qquad\text{so}\qquad \lambda_k^{\mathrm{race}}\approx\frac{h_k(p_0)}{\log(p_0/a_0)} .
\label{eq:lamrace}
\end{equation}
This is far below the static threshold $c_k^2/(4\sigma_k)$ of \cref{prop:fixed}(b), and it depends on the initial scale and the predictor gain, neither of which enters the static threshold.
For example, with $c_k=\sigma_k=p_0=1$ and $a_0=10^{-4}$, $\lambda_k^{\mathrm{race}}\approx0.05$, five times smaller than the static value $0.25$.
For $\alpha\ne1$ the logarithmic corrections of the expansion are comparable to $\log(1/a_0)$ (\cref{app:proofs}), so we use \eqref{eq:race}.

Without the stop-gradient, or without a predictor when the views have equal marginals, every direction decays, as known in substance \citep{tian2021understanding,halvagal2023implicit}; heavy Bernoulli masking is an exception to the second, and without \cref{ass:aligned} it takes a general linear-response form (\cref{app:more}).
